# p4-approachb -- boot Apple's BaseSystem unmerged, ESD alongside.
#
# ORIGINAL HEADER:
# p4-linuxmedia -- boot the media we built on Linux, against a blank disk.
#
# The question this answers: can Linux build working Mavericks media? The
# content matches the Mac-produced reference file-for-file, but content
# matching is not the same as booting.
#
# ORIGINAL HEADER:
# p3-full -- ONE VARIABLE from p3-oc: the firmware.
#
# With this, NOTHING in the boot path is Tier 2. OpenCore is built from
# pinned source offline; so now is the firmware, out of the same pinned
# EDK II tree (acidanthera/audk), by boot/build-ovmf.sh. This is the profile
# P3 exists to produce, and `bin/tier-check.sh --strict` passes with it.
#
# This file previously named Debian's stock OVMF 2024.02. That firmware does
# not work with OpenCore on this host -- OpenCore runs, renders nothing, and
# never boots macOS, and it fails identically with khronokernel's reference
# OpenCore 0.6.6, so it is not our build. See the P3 Task 8 entry in
# NOTES.md for the full diagnosis, and Task 9 for this replacement.
#
# Two setup steps this profile depends on, in order:
#
#   ./boot/build-ovmf.sh          produces the CODE/VARS pair below
#   ./boot/make-nvram.sh p3-full  gives this VM its own writable copy of
#                                 VARS, which is the file it will scribble
#                                 EFI variables into
#
# YOU MUST PRESS A KEY. This profile does not boot unattended.
#
# config.plist sets Misc > Boot > Timeout = 5, and with an empty NVRAM the
# picker's default is entry 1, "EFI (external)" -- the OpenCore disk itself.
# Let the five seconds run out and OpenCore boots that, re-entering
# EFI/BOOT/BOOTx64.efi, which fails with EFI_ALREADY_STARTED and hangs.
# Press 2 (Mavericks) within five seconds of the picker appearing:
#
#   ./vm/run.sh p3-full &
#   # then, over the monitor socket, repeatedly:  sendkey 2
#
# Two traps for anyone automating this. The picker text STAYS ON SCREEN
# after the timeout has already fired and chosen for you, so a late
# screenshot of a picker is not proof that the picker is still waiting --
# look for the word "Timeout" at the end of the prompt line. And a
# screendump of the picker has exactly TWO distinct colours (white text on
# black, 0.35% non-black pixels), so a "how many colours" heuristic reads it
# as a blank screen. A genuinely blank screen has one colour.
#
# This is a config.plist defect, not a firmware one. See the P3 Task 9
# addendum in NOTES.md.
#
# ORIGINAL HEADER:
# p3-oc -- ONE VARIABLE: our own OpenCore, built from pinned source.
#
# Everything else is held constant against p2-clone: same reference
# firmware via -bios, same chipset, same CPU, same disks. So a failure here
# is our OpenCore build or our config.plist, and nothing else.
#
# The bootloader is no longer Tier 2 -- but the firmware still is, which is
# why tier-check will keep flagging this profile until p3-full.
#
# This is the template every later experiment copies: include the baseline,
# override exactly the lines that differ, change nothing else. The diff IS
# the experiment.
#
# Here the only differences from p1-reference are the target disk (a clone
# instead of the original) and a headless display with a monitor socket, so
# boot can be timed and screenshotted from the host without a window.
#
# p1-reference still names the installer ISO and the original disk, so this
# profile cannot simply @include it -- a profile is a flat list of arguments
# and QEMU would see two -drive entries with id=target. Until the hardware
# profile is split further, this one spells out its own drives.

@include base-kvm

-machine
q35,vmport=off
-cpu
Penryn,+ssse3,+sse4.1,+sse4.2

# Our own OVMF, 4MB split CODE/VARS as a pflash pair.
#
# P1 tried pflash once and got a permanently black screen -- but that was
# with the REFERENCE firmware, which is a complete -bios image, not the CODE
# half of a pair. Ours is genuinely split: 3,653,632 + 540,672 = 4,194,304,
# and QEMU sizes each flash device from its file, so the two have to add up.
# Do not "fix" this back to -bios. The combined OVMF.fd the same build
# produces is there if a -bios experiment is ever wanted.
#
# This is also what restores EFI variable persistence, which -bios has
# denied us since P1: the VARS half is writable and survives a power cycle.
# It is NOT the build's template -- boot/make-nvram.sh copies that here, and
# refuses to hand out the template itself.
-drive
if=pflash,format=raw,unit=0,readonly=on,file=%BUILD%/firmware/OVMF_CODE.fd
-drive
if=pflash,format=raw,unit=1,file=%IMAGES%/work/p3-full-VARS.fd

-device
ich9-usb-ehci1,id=usb,bus=pcie.0,addr=0x1d.7,multifunction=on
-device
ich9-usb-uhci1,masterbus=usb.0,firstport=0,bus=pcie.0,addr=0x1d.0,multifunction=on
-device
ich9-usb-uhci2,masterbus=usb.0,firstport=2,bus=pcie.0,addr=0x1d.1
-device
ich9-usb-uhci3,masterbus=usb.0,firstport=4,bus=pcie.0,addr=0x1d.2

# usb-storage, exactly as p3-oc has it. The IDE workaround this profile
# carried for stock OVMF -- which never enumerated the OpenCore image over
# USB at all -- is gone: our OVMF finds it over USB, like the reference
# firmware did. That was a firmware difference, and this is the firmware
# that does not have it.
-drive
id=opencore,if=none,format=raw,file=%IMAGES%/work/opencore-p3.img
-device
usb-storage,bus=usb.0,drive=opencore

-drive
id=target,if=none,format=qcow2,file=%IMAGES%/work/p4-target.qcow2
-device
ide-hd,bus=ide.0,drive=target

# usb-net, deliberately, even though e1000-82545em is now the default
# everywhere else (docs/open-questions.md Q2, docs/decisions/0008).
#
# A NIC IS BUILD-TIME STATE IN 10.9: the OS records the interfaces it has
# seen in /Library/Preferences/SystemConfiguration, and an already-installed
# system meeting a different NIC brings up no interface for it at all --
# measured 2026-09-21, both directions. The target disk this profile names
# was installed with usb-net, so usb-net is what it can use. Changing this
# line without rebuilding that disk produces a guest with no network.
-netdev
user,id=net0
-device
usb-net,bus=usb.0,netdev=net0

-device
usb-kbd,bus=usb.0
-device
usb-mouse,bus=usb.0

-device
VGA,vgamem_mb=64

-display
none
-monitor
unix:%IMAGES%/work/monitor.sock,server,nowait

# APPROACH B: do not merge the media at all.
#
# Apple's BaseSystem.dmg is a complete HFS+ filesystem with Apple's own
# ownership. Converting it to raw and booting it directly means we never
# create a filesystem, never mount one to populate it, and therefore never
# need root -- which is what sank the merged build: launchd refused every
# daemon with "Dubious ownership", because udisks mounts as uid 1000 and
# rsync could not preserve root.
#
# The ESD rides along as a second disk, mounting as "OS X Install ESD",
# carrying Packages. The design recorded as a hypothesis that the installer
# finds its packages there; this profile tests it.
-drive
id=basesystem,if=none,format=raw,file=%IMAGES%/media/work/basesystem-apple.img
-device
ide-hd,bus=ide.1,drive=basesystem
-drive
id=esd,if=none,format=raw,file=%IMAGES%/media/work/esd.img
-device
ide-hd,bus=ide.2,drive=esd
